{#
  The provider fabric's L2 domain: a second libvirt network alongside the
  lab's existing 'default' (virbr0).

  An ISOLATED network - no <forward> element at all - and deliberately with
  no <ip>. Both halves of that matter, and the combination is the only one
  libvirt actually allows for what this needs to be:

  no <ip>, and therefore no <dhcp> and no <dns>
      Every address on this network is static, configured by NMState on the
      OCP nodes and by the containerlab FRR configs on the fabric side. The
      decisive consequence is that libvirt starts NO dnsmasq for this
      network: a network with an address gets one whether or not DHCP is
      configured, and a second DHCP/DNS server on a bridge that three
      OpenShift nodes are plugged into is the kind of thing that ruins an
      afternoon and is miserable to diagnose. Leaving the host itself off the
      fabric also keeps the "provider network" genuinely external, which is
      the point of the simulation - the host talks to the nodes and to the
      containerlab VM over virbr0, as it always has.

  no <forward>
      <forward mode='open'/> looks like the better choice here, because open
      mode adds no firewall rules at all, whereas an isolated network can get

        -A FORWARD -i virbr1 -j REJECT
        -A FORWARD -o virbr1 -j REJECT

      which would matter if anything ever loaded br_netfilter and started
      pushing bridged frames through FORWARD. But libvirt REQUIRES an IP
      address for open mode and refuses to define the network without one:

        XML error: open forwarding requested, but no IP address provided

      so 'open plus no address' does not exist. Between 'open with an address
      on the host' and 'isolated with none', isolated wins: the dnsmasq
      problem above is worse than the REJECT problem, and the REJECT problem
      is narrower than it first appears -

        * libvirt builds those rules around the network's IP range. With no
          <ip> there is no range and nothing to build them from.
        * In clab_deploy_mode 'vm' - the default - Docker lives inside the
          containerlab VM, so br_netfilter is never loaded on the hypervisor
          and bridged frames on virbr1 do not traverse FORWARD at all.
        * In 'host' mode Docker is on the hypervisor and does load it, but
          containerlab inserts an ACCEPT for every bridge its topology
          references, and fabric-network.yml inserts an explicit intra-bridge
          ACCEPT above anything libvirt may have added.

      fabric-network.yml prints whatever rules do reference this bridge after
      the network starts, so the assumption is checked rather than trusted.

  MTU {{ clab_fabric_mtu }}
      The fabric carries Geneve (phases 1-3) or VXLAN (phase 4) wrapped
      around pod traffic that is itself already at the cluster MTU. At 1500
      the BGP sessions come up fine and then large flows black-hole, which is
      a miserable thing to debug. libvirt applies this to the bridge and to
      every tap it creates on it; the guests still need their own MTU set,
      which the NMState policies and the containerlab topology both do.

  STP stays on (libvirt's default) - harmless here, and the phase-4 EVPN
  fabric puts more than one device on this bridge.
#}
<network>
  <name>{{ clab_fabric_network }}</name>
  <bridge name='{{ clab_fabric_bridge }}' stp='on' delay='0'/>
  <mtu size='{{ clab_fabric_mtu }}'/>
</network>
